Article reference: aa58106-25
Title: VELOCE III. Reconstructing Radial Velocity Curves of Classical Cepheids
Author(s): Giordano Viviani, Richard I. Anderson
Journal: Astronomy & Astrophysics (A&A), 2026
J/A+A/                                                      (Viviani+, 2026)
================================================================================
VELOCE-III. Reconstructing radial velocity curves of classical Cepheids
    Giordano Viviani, Richard I. Anderson
================================================================================
ADC_Keywords: Radial velocities; Stars, variable; Binaries, spectroscopic;
Keywords: Stars: oscillations - Stars: variables: Cepheids -
          Techniques: radial velocities

Abstract:
  We present a novel framework for accurately reconstructing radial velocity
  (RV) curves of classical Cepheids (Cepheids) from sparsely sampled time-series
  data suitable for application in large spectroscopic surveys. The framework
  provides a prior for the principal components of RV curves established based
  on high-precision measurements from the VELOcities of CEpheids (VELOCE)
  project; template RV curves of Cepheids can be readily extracted from our
  results. We demonstrate the ability of our framework to estimate unbiased
  pulsation average velocities, v_ gamma, to within 20-30 m/s, and peak-to-peak
  amplitudes, P2P, to within ~1.1%. Subsampling the initial data set, we show
  that v_ gamma and P2P can be determined to within ~500 m/s and 4%,
  respectively, from as few as three observations drawn at random pulsation
  phases, with the phase of minimum radius fitted rather than assumed. As
  expected, the precision on both v_gamma and P2P improves markedly with the
  number of observations, N_RV; the precision on P2P stabilizes beyond
  N_RV~10-15, whereas that on v_gamma continues to improve. We fitted existing
  time-series RV data of Cepheids in the Large and Small Magellanic Clouds (LMC,
  SMC) using this framework and obtained typical root mean square errors of
  0.5-2.0 km/s. The typical total uncertainty on v_gamma achieved for the SMC
  Cepheids is ~0.84 km/s, providing sensitivity to spectroscopic binaries (SB).
  We identified 8 SB1 systems; two and one of which are new detections in the
  LMC and SMC, respectively. This yields a single-lined SB fraction of ~25% and
  27% in the two galaxies, similar to the Milky Way's SB fraction of 29%,
  established as part of VELOCE. Despite their relatively small number, LMC
  Cepheids reproduce the known line-of-sight component of the LMC's large-scale
  rotation, which differs in the extremes by more than 80km/s. The kinematics of
  the SMC are more complex and not sufficiently sampled by the available
  Cepheids. Our framework is designed to yield accurate v_gamma and P2P of
  Cepheids observed by large spectroscopic surveys, such as 4MOST, SDSS-V, and
  others, and will unlock new insights into the kinematics and multiplicity of
  evolved intermediate-mass stellar populations.

Description:
  A principal component analysis (PCA) was applied to the Fourier-series models
  of the radial velocity (RV) curves of 219 Galactic classical Cepheids from
  VELOCE DR1 (Anderson et al., 2024A&A...686A.177A), sampled on a uniform grid
  of 1000 phase points in [0,1), with phase 0 at minimum radius. The PCA was
  computed on a randomly selected training set  (85% of the stars) after
  subtracting the mean training-set curve, Vmean; the remaining stars form the
  test set. The first six principal components (PCs), which explain ~99.2% of
  the variance, were retained. An RV curve is then modelled as V(phi) = vgamma +
  Vmean(phi+dphi) + sum_{i=1}^{6} p_i PC_i(phi+dphi). File pca.dat lists Vmean
  and the six PCs on the phase grid. File coeffs.dat lists, for each Cepheid,
  log P, the training/test membership, the PC coefficients p_i obtained by
  fitting its VELOCE-I model, and the phase offset dphi between the Gaia DR3
  G-band reference epoch and minimum radius. File kde.dat lists the bandwidths
  and domains of the kernel density estimates (KDE) that defines the priors
  P(p_1,...,p_6|log P) and P(dphi|log P). The priors can be rebuilt exactly from
  the training-set rows of coeffs.dat and kde.dat. File tableb1.dat (Table B.1)
  lists the results of applying the framework to literature RV time series of 22
  LMC Cepheids (Storm et al.,2011A&A...534A..95S) and 24 SMC Cepheids (Gieren et
  al., 2018A&A...620A..99G).

